    'content' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Nobody tells you that friendship gets harder after school. Once you start working, paying rent and maybe raising children, the easy daily contact of campus disappears. If you have been wondering how to make friends as an adult, you are in good company, and the answer is simpler than it feels. It takes a bit of intention, a bit of courage and a lot of small repeated moments.</p>
<p>This guide gives you practical steps that fit a busy Kenyan life, from the matatu stage to the office, the estate WhatsApp group and the weekend nyama choma. No tricks, only habits that work.</p>

<h2>Why adult friendships feel harder</h2>
<p>At school, you saw the same faces every day, with free time and shared problems. As an adult, your schedule is crowded, your friends are spread across towns, and many people are tired by evening. Friendships do not form on their own anymore. Someone has to send the first message, suggest the first meal and remember to follow up.</p>
<p>The encouraging part is that almost everyone feels this. Many of the people you see at work or at church would love an extra friend and are simply waiting for someone else to start. That someone can be you.</p>

<h2>How to make friends as an adult: start with who you already know</h2>
<p>Before searching for strangers, look at the people already around you. Colleagues, neighbours, old schoolmates, cousins, friends of friends and the parents at your child's school are all warm leads. A friendship built on a small existing connection is much easier than starting from zero.</p>
<ul>
<li>Message an old classmate you liked and suggest catching up over tea.</li>
<li>Invite a colleague to lunch instead of eating at your desk.</li>
<li>Ask a friend to bring a friend along to your next plan.</li>
<li>Greet your neighbours properly and offer a small kindness, like sharing something from the market.</li>
</ul>
<p>This costs nothing and often gives quick results. You only need one or two people to start, because they will introduce you to others.</p>

<h2>Choose places where you see the same people again</h2>
<p>A single event rarely creates a friendship, but a weekly activity can. Look for things that meet on a regular schedule so you see the same faces over several weeks. Repetition makes people comfortable.</p>
<ul>
<li>Sports and fitness: a running club, a Saturday football game, a gym class or a swimming group.</li>
<li>Faith and community: a church or mosque fellowship, a youth or women's group, a chama or welfare group.</li>
<li>Skills and hobbies: a book club, a photography walk, a cooking class, a choir or a language group.</li>
<li>Giving back: clean-up days, mentoring students, tree planting or community projects.</li>
</ul>
<p>Pick one and go consistently for a month before judging it. The first visit is always the most awkward. By the third or fourth, people will start saving you a seat.</p>

<h2>Make the first move, kindly and simply</h2>
<p>Most adults are waiting for the other person to speak first. You can break that pattern with one easy sentence. Compliment something real, ask a simple question, or comment on what you are both doing.</p>
<p>Some lines you can borrow: "Have you been coming here long?", "I am new to this group, any tips?" or "That is a great bag, where did you get it?" Keep your tone friendly and your body language open. If the chat goes well, take it one step further, with something like "I usually get tea after this, would you like to join?"</p>
<p>If you want ideas for chatting with people online as well as face to face, see our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-start-a-conversation-online">starting a conversation online</a> and the <a href="/blog/five-first-message-openers">five first message openers</a>. The same principles of being specific, warm and curious work everywhere.</p>

<h2>Invite people to do something specific</h2>
<p>Vague plans like "let us meet sometime" almost never happen. A specific, easy invitation is far more likely to get a yes. Name the activity, the place and the time, and keep the stakes low.</p>
<p>For example: "I am walking at Uhuru Gardens on Saturday morning, would you like to join?" or "A few of us are watching the game at a place near town on Sunday, come along." If they cannot make it, they will often suggest another time. If they do not, invite someone else and try again later. You are not being rejected, only matched with the right schedule.</p>

<h2>Be the person who follows up</h2>
<p>Most potential friendships fade because nobody follows up. After you have had a good chat, send a short message the same day or the next: "Nice meeting you today, I enjoyed our talk about football." It tells the other person you were glad to meet them, and it opens the door for the next plan.</p>
<p>Keep going gently. A voice note, a funny picture, or a question about something they mentioned last time keeps the connection alive. Remember small details such as a job interview, an exam or a sick relative, and ask how it went. This is how acquaintances become friends.</p>

<h2>Give friendship time and room</h2>
<p>Friendship deepens through time spent together, small favours and honest conversation. You cannot rush it. Be reliable: arrive on time, keep your promises, and let people know when plans change. Be generous with your attention, put your phone away when you are with someone, and let them finish their story.</p>
<p>Share a little of yourself, too. People feel closer when they see the real you, including your worries and your humour. At the same time, take your time with very personal details, and trust your instincts about who has earned that closeness.</p>

<h2>Make time, even when life is busy</h2>
<p>Busy people often say they have no time for friends, but friendship does not need much, only consistency. Pair it with something you already do. Walk with a friend instead of walking alone, run errands together, or catch up by voice note while you cook. A thirty minute tea once a week can keep a friendship alive better than a big outing twice a year.</p>
<p>Protect one slot in your week for people, the way you protect a meeting at work. It could be Saturday morning, Wednesday lunch or Sunday after church. When the slot is already in your diary, it becomes much easier to say yes, and your friends learn that you are someone who shows up.</p>

<h2>Meeting people online, sensibly</h2>
<p>Apps and websites are now a normal way to meet people, including for friendship. If you live in a place where you do not know many people, they can help you find others near you who want company too. You can <a href="/browse">browse profiles</a> on ConnectHub, read what people share about their interests and start a chat. A clear profile helps others know what you are looking for, so see our tips on <a href="/blog/how-to-write-a-dating-profile-bio">writing a bio that sounds like you</a>.</p>
<p>Take the same care you would anywhere. Do not send money, do not share your home address early, and meet in a busy public place the first time. Read our <a href="/safety-tips">safety tips</a> and the guide to <a href="/blog/meet-someone-safely">meeting someone safely</a> before you go.</p>

<h2>When it does not click, move on kindly</h2>
<p>Not every person will become a friend, and that is fine. Some people will be busy, some will not match your energy, and some will drift away. Do not take it as proof that something is wrong with you. Keep your circle of acquaintances wide, and let friendships deepen with the people who respond warmly.</p>
<p>If you are feeling low or isolated and it is lasting, talk to someone you trust or a counsellor. You can also read our article on <a href="/blog/making-friends-in-a-new-town">making friends in a new town</a> if you have recently moved. Start with one message to one person this week, and let that small step lead to the next.</p>
